= Simulated reality — Technical =

'''Simulated reality''' is an ontological hypothesis according to which the observer-accessible world is implemented by an underlying artificial process, commonly imagined as computation. The hypothesis belongs primarily to metaphysics and epistemology unless a specific implementation makes discriminating empirical predictions.

The modern '''simulation argument''' formulated by Nick Bostrom is an anthropic and probabilistic argument, not an empirical detection method.<ref name="bostrom">[https://doi.org/10.1111/1467-9213.00309 Bostrom, N. (2003), “Are We Living in a Computer Simulation?”, ''The Philosophical Quarterly'' 53(211), 243–255.]</ref>


The simulated-reality hypothesis is primarily a question in [[Metaphysics]] and [[Epistemology]], because it concerns what underlies experienced reality and what evidence could distinguish alternatives. Its observer-counting assumptions depend on theories of [[Consciousness]], while its implementation claims depend on what kinds of [[Computation]] are physically possible.

== Ontological hypothesis ==

Let <math>W</math> denote the observer-accessible world and <math>B</math> a proposed deeper-level system.

A simulated-reality hypothesis asserts that there exists some implementation relation

<math>I(B)\mapsto W</math>

such that the states and causal regularities accessible inside <math>W</math> are generated or realized by <math>B</math>.

This leaves multiple questions open:

* Is <math>B</math> computational?
* Is <math>W</math> discretized?
* Is every physical degree of freedom represented?
* Are observers conscious?
* Is simulation exact, approximate, or demand-driven?
* What laws govern <math>B</math>?

Therefore “simulation hypothesis” denotes a family of models rather than one complete physical theory.

== Epistemic indistinguishability ==

Let <math>H_S</math> be a simulation hypothesis and <math>H_B</math> a base-reality hypothesis.

For evidence <math>E</math>, the hypotheses are observationally indistinguishable over an evidence class when

<math>P(E|H_S)=P(E|H_B).</math>

If this equality holds for every accessible observation, no internal experiment can discriminate the hypotheses.

This is an underdetermination relation.

It does not imply equal prior probability.

== Bayesian formulation ==

Posterior odds are

<math>\frac{P(H_S|E)}{P(H_B|E)}
=
\frac{P(E|H_S)}{P(E|H_B)}
\frac{P(H_S)}{P(H_B)}.</math>

If the likelihood ratio is approximately one, evidence leaves prior odds nearly unchanged.

If a proposed simulation model predicts a distinctive artifact, then

<math>\Lambda(E)=\frac{P(E|H_S)}{P(E|H_B)}</math>

can differ from one and the observation can update belief.

The broad unconstrained hypothesis “some simulator generated everything we observe” supplies no unique likelihood model.

== Bostrom's structure ==

Bostrom considers civilizations capable of reaching a technologically mature posthuman stage and running large numbers of ancestor simulations.

The argument concludes that at least one of three propositions is true:

# the fraction of civilizations reaching the relevant posthuman stage is extremely small;
# the fraction of posthuman civilizations interested in running substantial numbers of ancestor simulations is extremely small;
# the fraction of observers with experiences like ours who are simulated is extremely large.<ref name="bostrom" />

The argument is conditional on assumptions connecting technological capability, simulation practices, consciousness, and observer selection.

Bostrom and Kulczycki subsequently identified a mathematical non sequitur in the 2003 derivation and published two repairs that preserve the broad trilemma conclusion.<ref name="patch">[https://doi.org/10.1093/analys/anq107 Bostrom, N. and Kulczycki, M. (2011), “A Patch for the Simulation Argument,” ''Analysis'' 71(1), 54–61.]</ref> Quantitative treatments should therefore use the repaired argument rather than silently reproducing the original derivation.

== Simplified observer-count model ==

Let:

* <math>C</math> = number of relevant biological civilizations;
* <math>f_p</math> = fraction reaching posthuman capability;
* <math>\bar N</math> = mean number of relevant simulations run by each capable civilization;
* <math>n_s</math> = mean relevant observers per simulation;
* <math>n_r</math> = mean relevant nonsimulated observers per civilization.

Then expected simulated observers are

<math>N_S=Cf_p\bar N n_s</math>

and nonsimulated observers are approximately

<math>N_R=Cn_r.</math>

Under a simple random-observer assumption,

<math>P(S)=\frac{f_p\bar N n_s}{f_p\bar N n_s+n_r}.</math>

If <math>f_p\bar N n_s\gg n_r</math>, this ratio approaches one.

But every term is uncertain, and the sampling assumption itself is philosophical.

== Reference classes ==

The random-observer step requires a reference class <math>R</math>.

Possible definitions include:

<math>R_1</math> = all conscious observers,

<math>R_2</math> = human-like observers,

<math>R_3</math> = observers with our evidence,

<math>R_4</math> = observers at our technological stage.

Changing <math>R</math> changes the counts.

This is a general reference-class problem in anthropic reasoning.

Weatherson argued that several interpretations of the relevant indifference principle fail to support Bostrom's intended inference without additional assumptions.<ref name="weatherson">[https://doi.org/10.1111/1467-9213.00323 Weatherson, B. (2003), “Are You a Sim?”, ''The Philosophical Quarterly'' 53(212), 425–431.]</ref> Bostrom responded that the criticism mischaracterized the appropriate principle.<ref name="reply">[https://doi.org/10.1111/j.0031-8094.2005.00387.x Bostrom, N. (2005), “The Simulation Argument: Reply to Weatherson,” ''The Philosophical Quarterly'' 55(218), 90–97.]</ref>

== Conscious-observer assumption ==

Observer counting requires that simulated entities can be conscious.

Let <math>C(x)</math> denote the claim that implementation <math>x</math> supports conscious experience.

The simulation argument needs non-negligible probability for

<math>C(x_{sim})=1.</math>

This is not established merely by behavioral equivalence.

If consciousness depends on substrate-specific biological or physical properties, then high-fidelity functional simulation may fail to create additional conscious observers.

If consciousness is organizationally or computationally invariant, simulated observers may be possible.

The argument therefore imports unresolved questions from philosophy of mind and neuroscience.

== Computational resources ==

A physical simulation consumes finite resources.

Let:

* <math>R_c</math> = compute operations per unit time;
* <math>R_m</math> = memory;
* <math>R_i</math> = input/output bandwidth;
* <math>E</math> = energy.

Required resources depend on model resolution and architecture:

<math>Cost=F(N_{state},\Delta t,precision,interaction\ structure).</math>

A brute-force microscopic simulation may be infeasible while a coarse-grained observer-level simulation might be cheaper.

Without a concrete simulator model, resource estimates vary over many orders of magnitude.

== Selective rendering ==

A speculative simulator could compute only states causally relevant to observers.

This resembles level-of-detail optimization in graphics.

However, physical interactions can propagate information across large systems, and future measurements may interrogate states not previously observed.

A selective-rendering proposal therefore needs a consistency mechanism.

Invoking selective rendering only to evade resource objections does not provide independent evidence.

== Reversible and quantum computation ==

Advanced computation proposals sometimes invoke reversible computing or quantum computing.

These technologies change resource tradeoffs but do not establish that arbitrary conscious worlds can be simulated efficiently.

A quantum computer can accelerate specific classes of computation; it does not make every exponential problem tractable.

Resource feasibility must be demonstrated for the actual model.

== Scientific simulation ==

A scientific simulation study normally has:

* target system <math>T</math>;
* conceptual model <math>M</math>;
* numerical or computational implementation <math>A</math>;
* executable code <math>C</math>;
* output <math>O</math>.

The philosophy of simulation distinguishes questions of implementation correctness from questions of model adequacy.<ref name="sep">[https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/simulations-science/ Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Computer Simulations in Science.”]</ref>

A cosmological simulation generated by researchers is epistemically accessible from outside its simulated world.

The simulated-reality hypothesis instead places the observer inside the target of interest with no established access to the implementation level.

== Verification and validation ==

For an ordinary simulation:

'''Code verification''' asks whether code implements the intended algorithm.

'''Solution verification''' asks whether numerical output approximates the intended mathematical solution adequately.

'''Validation''' asks whether the model represents the target system sufficiently for its purpose.<ref name="sep" />

These distinctions are useful for hypothetical simulated worlds too, but internal observers may lack access to the simulator's source model or validation target.

The existence of high-quality scientific simulation does not support the ontological simulation hypothesis by itself.

== Detectable artifacts ==

A specific simulator implementation may predict artifacts.

Possible classes include:

* lattice anisotropy;
* finite-precision effects;
* numerical instability;
* resource-management discontinuities;
* intervention events;
* inconsistent hidden state.

For candidate artifact <math>A</math>, scientific evidence requires a model that specifies

<math>P(A|H_S)</math>

and comparison models for

<math>P(A|H_i).</math>

Merely discovering an anomaly after the fact and labeling it a “glitch” is not a valid likelihood model.

== Discrete spacetime claims ==

Suppose physical theory implies a fundamental length <math>\ell</math> or discrete spectrum.

This gives

<math>Discreteness</math>

but not

<math>External computation.</math>

Digital simulations are often discrete, but discreteness has many possible physical interpretations.

The inference

<math>discrete\ physics\Rightarrow simulation</math>

is invalid without an additional premise tying discreteness uniquely to artificial computation.

== Information bounds ==

Finite entropy or information bounds are sometimes described as memory limits of a simulator.

Even if a region has maximum entropy <math>S_{max}</math>, an information bound

<math>I_{max}=S_{max}/(k_B\ln 2)</math>

does not identify external hardware.

It is a physical constraint within the theory.

Interpreting it as a computer-memory allocation is a metaphysical analogy unless independent evidence supports that ontology.

== “Laws as code” ==

Physical law

<math>\mathcal L(\phi,\partial\phi)=0</math>

can be represented computationally, but mathematical representability does not entail implementation as software.

Many different physical systems can satisfy the same equation.

The distinction is between:

'''description''' — equations model regularities;

'''implementation''' — another system executes a representation that realizes those regularities.

Evidence for description alone does not establish implementation.

== Falsifiable subclasses ==

Define constrained simulation hypothesis

<math>H_S(\theta)</math>

with implementation parameters <math>\theta</math>.

A prediction

<math>E\sim P(E|H_S(\theta))</math>

can be tested.

If observations have very low probability under <math>H_S(\theta)</math>, that parameterized model is disfavored.

But replacing it with unconstrained <math>H_S'</math> that predicts any result avoids falsification at the cost of explanatory content.

Scientific progress requires preserving the failed constraint rather than indefinitely widening the hypothesis.

== Model selection ==

A flexible simulation hypothesis may fit any dataset.

Model selection penalizes unnecessary flexibility.

In Bayesian form, marginal likelihood is

<math>P(E|H)=\int P(E|\theta,H)P(\theta|H)d\theta.</math>

A model that fits only through a huge unconstrained parameter space can have low evidential efficiency.

This does not prove nonsimulation, but it illustrates why “the simulator could choose anything” is not a strong scientific explanation.

== Prior specification ==

Assigning <math>P(H_S)</math> is difficult because there is no agreed frequency class of universes.

Possible prior considerations include:

* complexity of the hypothesis;
* technological extrapolation;
* metaphysical commitments;
* observer-selection assumptions.

Different priors can dominate when likelihoods are observationally equivalent.

Reporting a numerical posterior without exposing the prior is therefore misleading.

== Nested simulation model ==

Let simulation depth be <math>d</math> and branching factor <math>b_d</math>.

The number of simulations at level <math>d</math> could be

<math>N_d=N_0\prod_{k=0}^{d-1}b_k.</math>

Physical resource constraints likely make <math>b_k</math> and fidelity dependent on depth.

An infinite hierarchy is not implied unless resources or laws permit unbounded nesting.

Nested simulations change observer-count calculations and reference-class choices.

== Causal closure inside a simulation ==

A simulated world could have stable internal causal relations even if implemented externally.

Let internal transition function be

<math>x_{t+1}=F(x_t).</math>

External hardware implements <math>F</math>, but inhabitants can still correctly discover regularities of <math>F</math> through science.

Thus being simulated would not make internal science “fake.”

It would instead add an implementation-level explanation beneath the internal laws.

== Intervention by simulator ==

An external simulator might intervene:

<math>x_{t+1}=F(x_t)+I_t.</math>

A detectable intervention requires <math>I_t</math> to create observations inconsistent with internal models strongly enough to overcome alternative explanations such as measurement error or incomplete physics.

A singular unexplained event is weak evidence because internal model uncertainty is usually large.

Repeated structured interventions could be stronger if independently measured.

== Epistemic closure problem ==

If every internal observation is generated through the simulator, the simulator can in principle manipulate apparent evidence.

This creates a radical skeptical scenario similar to brain-in-a-vat arguments.

But radical skepticism concerns what can be known with certainty, whereas ordinary scientific reasoning works with comparative evidence and reliability.

Failure to obtain certainty that <math>H_S</math> is false does not entail positive evidence for <math>H_S</math>.

== Semantic externalism ==

Brain-in-a-vat discussions raise questions about whether the content of an agent's words depends partly on causal relations to its environment.

A permanently simulated agent may refer successfully to simulated trees, bodies, and space because those are the stable objects in its environment.

Thus “simulated” does not automatically mean “illusory” in every semantic sense.

This point motivates philosophical views in which virtual entities can be genuinely real at their own level.

== Virtual realism ==

Chalmers argues that virtual objects and worlds can be real rather than mere illusions and uses simulation scenarios to revisit traditional philosophical problems.<ref name="chalmers">[https://consc.net/reality/ Chalmers, D. J. (2022), ''Reality+: Virtual Worlds and the Problems of Philosophy''.]</ref>

This position is logically distinct from:

<math>P(our\ world\ is\ simulated).</math>

One can believe virtual objects are real without believing our world is virtual.

== Physicalism and substrate dependence ==

If mental states supervene on sufficiently detailed functional organization, simulated consciousness becomes more plausible.

If mental states depend on specific biological chemistry, quantum properties, or other substrate features, simulation requirements become more demanding.

Current consciousness science does not settle this issue.

Therefore a simulation argument that treats observer simulation as straightforward should mark the assumption explicitly.

== Simulation versus emulation ==

Engineering sometimes distinguishes:

'''simulation''' — reproduce selected behavior of a target model;

'''emulation''' — reproduce the operational interface or behavior closely enough to substitute for the original system.

A whole-world hypothesis can use either idea loosely.

Scientific analysis should specify fidelity requirements rather than rely on terminology.

== Ethical model ==

If simulated agent <math>i</math> has welfare <math>w_i</math> and probability of moral patienthood <math>p_i</math>, a precautionary expected-welfare analysis might weight

<math>E[W]=\sum_i p_i w_i.</math>

This is not a validated formula for moral value. It illustrates why uncertainty about consciousness does not automatically imply zero ethical concern.

The ethics remain conditional on the existence and morally relevant capacities of simulated beings.

== Explanatory regress ==

Let <math>L_0</math> be our world and <math>L_1</math> its hypothetical simulator.

Explaining <math>L_0</math> by <math>L_1</math> leaves open:

<math>Why(L_1)?</math>

A further simulator <math>L_2</math> can repeat the question.

Thus simulated reality is an implementation hypothesis, not automatically an ultimate explanation of existence.

== Research standards ==

A serious empirical simulation claim should provide:

* a precisely defined simulation model;
* implementation constraints;
* predicted observable signatures;
* competing hypotheses;
* preregistered or prospective tests where possible;
* quantified likelihoods;
* error analysis;
* independent replication.

Without these elements, claims remain philosophical speculation.

== Common reasoning errors ==

'''Possibility-to-probability leap''' — because simulation is conceivable, it is assumed likely.

'''Capability extrapolation''' — current virtual worlds are treated as proof of future universe-scale conscious simulation.

'''Reference-class hiding''' — observer sampling is used without specifying who is counted.

'''Consciousness assumption''' — simulated agents are counted as conscious without justification.

'''Anomaly labeling''' — unexplained events are called “glitches” after observation.

'''Code metaphor''' — mathematical laws are treated as literal software.

'''Unfalsifiable rescue''' — every failed prediction is explained by an omnipotent simulator.

== Epistemic assessment ==

The broad simulated-reality hypothesis is metaphysically possible under multiple frameworks, but empirical support is weak because unconstrained versions make few discriminating predictions.

Bostrom's argument is best understood as a conditional constraint on a set of future-civilization and observer-selection assumptions, not as a measurement of current reality.<ref name="bostrom" />

The scientifically responsible state is therefore uncertainty with explicit separation of logical possibility, anthropic reasoning, technological feasibility, and empirical evidence.

